  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Express $\\frac{10}{(2x+1)(x+3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{10}{(2x+1)(x+3)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $2x+1$ |  | $x+3$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{(2x+1)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(2x+1)(x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n10=A(x+3)-2(2x+1).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =A(-\\frac{1}{2}+3)-2(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 10 & =\\frac{5}{2}A \\\\ A & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{(2x+1)(x+3)}=\\frac{4}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{x+3}.\n$$",
